A||||10||MooCow||I need tips for Head Gaskets||||1||
Z||000000||MooCow||05-30-2001||06:34 PM||||Well as soem of you may know my head gasket blew and Ed Parks said if I bring it back down there he would fix it for free.  Well I really have no way of getting it down there, plus he is starting to get backed up so it would be a while before it would get fixed.  So I am going to do it myself with the help of my freinds dad who is a certified Mechanic.  So where can I buy what I need for the head gaskets cause I am going to replace both of them??  Also what book should I het?? I have the haynes one but it doesn't have how to do the head gasket anywhere in it.  Also any tips from anyone out there?? Think I could get it done in a weekend??<P>                            Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Seanh||05-30-2001||06:56 PM||||Don't know about finding about changing head gaskets in the Haynes, but look for removeing the cylinder head. It in there, thats the book I followed. I found it to be alot easier then I though, but it was still a ***** ! Just make sure you keep your bolts straigh, I had some trouble with this aspect. I find using a screw box, with all the little dividers in it works great. BTW, the Haynes manual says you can remove the valve covers before manifold, this can't be done! The upper and mid manifold have to come off before the valve covers come off. Get a gasket set from the Fiero Store, seeing as they include the valve cover gasket as which mine from Advance Auto did not. Its all pretty obvious what to do once you start taking things off. Email me if you have any questions, as head gaskets seem to be as common as oil changes with my Fieros. sean@kdsi.net||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||05-30-2001||08:15 PM||||You need to get a top end gasket set for your engine set-up. Depending on your configuration you may need to get pieces of another.<BR>If you have a pic of your power plant, shoot me an e-mail unless you used all the 3.4, intake and all. If you did all you'll need is one for the 3.4.||||reg||1
Z||000003||drednot||05-30-2001||08:15 PM||||better hope that the heads are not warped.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mark||05-30-2001||10:03 PM||||You may save a little cash by buying the complete top end gasket set, rather than two head gaskets and a plenum set.  Also, watch out; the Fel-Pro plenum set has cork valve cover gaskets.  These gaskets are not for the Fiero V6.  I had to buy them separately, but you may be able to reuse your old ones (they feel like they are made out of silicone rubber).  Finally, you should also get a new head bolt set (about $26 at AutoZone, maybe less from Fiero Store.<P>The big question is why did the head gasket fail?  You should have the heads checked for warping and cracks.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||05-30-2001||10:10 PM||||Moo,  I got my top end set from the Fiero store.  At the time, they beat anyone else's prices but run thru your local and online stores as things are always subject to change.  While the heads are off, check the condition of the valves and guides and talk w/ Ed to find out mileage on them.  There is no time to do head recondition except when they're off the car so...  also have the mating surfaces 'touched up' so you don't need to have any after thoughts about sealing.  If they have been reconditioned fairly recently then just change any and all gaskets that you have in the set, otherwise ask your friend's dad to point you in the direction of a good machine shop (good not necessarliy cheap).<BR>Most of all, have fun, learn and GL<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 05-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||MooCow||05-30-2001||11:04 PM||||So do I buy head gaskets for a 95 3.4 or for an 87 2.8?????  Also the engine only has 54,000 on it.  The head gasket blew cause I started the car up to many times and it never started so it kept fludding it.  I need to be able to get the parts by Saturday this weekend at the latest cause its my weekend off.  I would love to start it tomorrow and have all weekend to work on it.  I'll check out autozone tomorrow.  Also I was told to get some sealer?????  I am trying to do this all myslef without the help of my Friends dad, cause he can't start to help till after the 15th of June cause of his daughters graduation party.  What tools am I going to need???????<P>p.s the engine looks just like a Fiero 2.8<P>                              Thanks Ken||||reg||1
Z||000007||Taijiguy||05-30-2001||11:27 PM||||Here's the best head gastket tip ever:<BR>Just before putting the heads back on, paint both sides of the gasket with a heavy coat chrome paint, and put it together with the paint still wet. It'll help everything seal up.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||05-31-2001||12:17 AM||||Ken,<BR>You'll need the head gaskets for the 3.4, but the intake gaskets for the 2.8.<P>How did the motor being flooded cause the head gasket to blow? <BR>Did someone with the proper tools and experience diagnose this?  <BR>Just curious.<BR>If it were me, I'd wait until I had the friend's dad around. If he's a certified mechanic, he knows what to look for. He can probably just look at stuff as the engine is being disassembled, and know what's going on. Will probably save you a lot of trouble, in the long run. You can learn from his experience.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||05-31-2001||02:07 AM||||Get what Raydar said, get Victor gaskets they have the proper rubber valve cover gaskets.<BR>Make sure to check prices on either a top end set for the 2.8 vrs. separate gaskets, might be cheaper one way or the other.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cozmo||05-31-2001||09:05 AM||||Hey MooCow,<BR>You might want to do some tests before you leap into tearing the top end apart...<BR>Definitely do a compression test, (maybe even a coolant pressure test also), that will tell you a lot.  Take off the thermostat cap and remove it, listen there for air escaping when you do the compression test.  You should also do a leakdown test and a vacuum test, they can also tell you A LOT about the overall health of the engine.<BR>  When I did mine last month I found cyl.#2 leaking into the water jacket using these tests, sure enough when I tore it down that's what it was.<BR>  If you do have a blown gasket, this gives you lots of opportunities to improve things while you're in there.  Take the heads to a machine shop to be leak tested and have the valves done.  Gasket match the intake and exhaust manifolds. Replace those pesky exhaust manifold bolts with Saturn studs and nuts.  I tore mine down in one weekend, dropped the heads off on Mon. picked them up on Fri. and re-assembled the engine the following weekend.<BR>   Take your time, do it right and you'll not only fix it but it'll run a lot better!<BR><B>C02M0</B>||||reg||1
